## Background

### Forge in this app

- The app uses `@ministryofjustice/hmpps-forge` (^0.4.4). Journeys are packaged with `createForgePackage({ journey, functions })` and registered in `src/app.ts` with `forge.registerPackage(pkg, deps)`. Dependencies are injected at that point.
- Forge does **not** keep answers between requests. On POST, answers come from the request body. Anything that must persist has to be written by our own effects (functions the journey runs when a step is accessed or submitted).
- The existing journeys are tied to this app in several ways. The eligibility module must not be:
  - SiLAS auth (`requireSilasAuth`)
  - `/case/:caseId` parameters
  - `ctx.getState("authenticatedAxios")`
  - `/call_centre/api/v1/` paths
  - app layouts
  - English text hard-coded in `block.ts` files

### The CCQ journey

CCQ is in `~/dev/laa-check-client-qualifies`; this summary was taken at commit `1a30ea3f`.

- **Step list:** the step-to-form map is in `app/controllers/flow/handler.rb`. The step list is rebuilt from the session on every request, from six sections in a fixed order: NonFinancial, Income, Partner, Outgoings, Property, AssetsAndVehicles (`app/lib/steps/helper.rb:72-79`).
- **Branching:** all of it comes from one set of predicates in `app/lib/steps/logic.rb`. The main ones are `controlled?`, `client_under_eighteen?`, `under_eighteen_no_means_test_required?`, `immigration_or_asylum?`, `asylum_supported?`, `non_means_tested?`, `passported?`, `skip_income_questions?`, `skip_capital_questions?` and `partner?`.
- **Size:** there are 41 possible steps. Six of them are add-another lists on a single page (incomes, benefits, dependant incomes, additional properties, bank accounts, vehicles).
- **Early exits:**
  - "Cannot use this service": shared ownership where the landlord is not the only other joint owner, or additional property in shared ownership.
  - An early gross-income result after `other_income`.
- **Check your answers:** has a change loop. Edits go into a pending copy that is committed when the loop ends, and the user is walked through any steps that become relevant.
- **CFE (Check Financial Eligibility) calls:**
  - `POST v6/assessments` for the assessment
  - `GET state_benefit_type` for benefit autocomplete

  About ten builder services assemble the payload from the relevant steps only. The result is `eligible`, `contribution_required` or `ineligible`.
- **Welsh:** there is no Welsh UI. Only the downloadable controlled work (CW) PDF forms have Welsh versions.

#### Step order

| # | Step | Shown when |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `client_age` | Always |
| 2 | `level_of_help` | Always |
| 3–7 | `under_18_clr`, `aggregated_means`, `how_to_aggregate`, `regular_income`, `under_eighteen_assets` | Controlled work and under 18. A chain: any "yes" ends it |
| 8 | `domestic_abuse_applicant` | Certificated work and means-tested |
| 9–10 | `immigration_or_asylum`, `immigration_or_asylum_type` | Controlled work |
| 11 | `immigration_or_asylum_type_upper_tribunal` | Certificated work and not a domestic abuse applicant |
| 12 | `asylum_support` | Immigration or asylum matter |
| 13 | `applicant` (partner, passporting benefit) | Means-tested |
| 14–16 | `dependant_details`, `dependant_income`, `dependant_income_details` | Means-tested and not passported |
| 17–21 | `employment_status`, `income`, `benefits`, `benefit_details`, `other_income` | Not skipping income questions |
| 22–27 | `partner_details`, `partner_employment_status`, `partner_income`, `partner_benefits`, `partner_benefit_details`, `partner_other_income` | Has a partner. If passported, only `partner_details` |
| 28–29 | `outgoings`, `partner_outgoings` | Not skipping income questions |
| 30–32 | `property`, `property_landlord`, and one of the housing-cost steps | Not skipping capital questions. Housing costs depend on ownership type |
| 33–37 | `property_entry`, `additional_property`(`_details`), `partner_additional_property`(`_details`) | Not skipping capital questions |
| 38–39 | `assets`, `partner_assets` | Not skipping capital questions |
| 40–41 | `vehicle`, `vehicles_details` | Certificated work only |

The full predicate code is in `app/lib/steps/logic.rb`, and each section's rules are in `app/lib/steps/*_section.rb`.

#### Out of scope for the module

These CCQ features belong to individual services, not the shared journey:

- CW form PDFs
- LAA Portal SAML sign-in
- analytics
- the journey logger
- result PDF download

A host can provide its own versions through the `ResultHandler` hook described below.

### How cla_backend works out eligibility today

Checked against `cla_backend` at `94801bbe` (7 September 2026).

**What it does.** Every save through the eligibility check API recalculates eligibility. `EligibilityCheckSerializerBase.save` calls `EligibilityCheck.update_state()` (`apps/legalaid/serializers.py`). That calls `EligibilityChecker`, which posts to CFE, then stores two things on the check:

- `state`: `yes`, `no` or `unknown`
- `calculations`: amounts in pence, built from CFE's response by `_translate_response`

A client cannot set `state` or `calculations`. `state` is listed in the call centre serializer's fields, but `update_state()` overwrites it on every save. The `is_eligible` endpoints (`apps/legalaid/views.py`, `apps/checker/views.py`) call CFE again without saving.

**Why it was built like this.**

- **2014: the calculator ran inside cla_backend.** cla_backend owned the means test for both of its front ends, cla_frontend (operators) and cla_public (the public checker). The front ends were thin and saved answers section by section. The calculation was cheap and in-process, so `state` was treated as a field derived from the answers and recalculated on every save. That kept it correct and showed `unknown` until there were enough answers. Commit `4f65c885` (June 2014) moved the recalculation from the model save to the API save, because it depends on related records (income, savings, property).
- **January 2024: CFE replaced the calculator's internals.** PR #1082 (`f82a3186`) changed `EligibilityChecker` to call CFE, so the Eligibility Platform team would own the calculation and its thresholds. PR #1103 (`121626de`) deleted the old calculator. Both kept the existing interface, callers and storage. As a result, what used to be a cheap in-process calculation became an HTTP call to CFE on every save. That was a side effect of keeping the change small, not a deliberate choice.

**What depends on the stored result.** This is why it cannot simply be dropped:

- MI reports read `eligibility_check.state`: `MIScopeReport`, `MIExtractByOutcome`, `MIExtractByOutcomeExtended`, `MIDemographicReport`, `MIProblemCategorisation`, `MICB1sSLA1`, `MICB1sSLA2`, `MICB1sSLAAgilisys` and `metrics.sql`.
- The OBIEE export (`reports/sql/obiee/export_legal_aid_eligibility_check.sql`) includes `calculations`.
- Specialist providers get `calculations` through `ExtendedEligibilityCheckSerializer` (`apps/cla_provider/serializers.py`).
- Each save logs a `means_test` event against the case, recording `state`. The case log context also includes it.

To meet D9, this module's result must fill `state` and `calculations` in the same shape, without cla_backend calling CFE again (Q3).

## Design

### Module layout

```
src/eligibility/                 ← the reusable unit: copy this folder to reuse
  index.ts                       ← public API; the only file hosts may import
  README.md                      ← how to mount, what to provide, options
  domain/
    logic.ts                     ← port of Steps::Logic
    sections/*.ts                ← port of the six section classes → stepsFor(answers)
    navigation.ts                ← nextStep, previousStep, relevantAnswers, cannotUseService
    cfe/*.ts                     ← port of the CFE payload builders → buildAssessment(answers)
    result.ts                    ← port of CfeResult / CalculationResult
  ports/                         ← AnswerStore, Assessor, ResultHandler interfaces
  adapters/                      ← SessionAnswerStore, CfeHttpClient
  journey/                       ← createEligibilityPackage(opts); one folder per step
  registries/                    ← Eligibility.* effects and conditions
  views/eligibility/*.njk        ← extend a layout supplied by the host
  locales/en.json
  tests/

src/journeys/eligibility-host/   ← cla-operator-app specific; NOT copied
  index.ts                       ← mounts the module with SiLAS auth, case param, layout
  resultHandler.ts               ← saves the result against the case
```

### Public API

```ts
const eligibilityPkg = createEligibilityPackage({
  path: "eligibility",                         // relative to the parent journey
  onAccess: [requireSilasAuth],                // host's auth guard
  checkId: Params("caseId"),                   // how this host identifies a check
  presetAnswers: { level_of_help: "controlled" }, // steps fully answered here are skipped
  onComplete: Format("/case/%1/eligibility-result", Params("caseId")),
  template: { layout: "layouts/operator.njk" },
});

forge.registerPackage<EligibilityDeps>(eligibilityPkg, {
  assessor: new CfeHttpClient(config.cfe),
  resultHandler: new CaseResultHandler(apiService),
  // answerStore defaults to SessionAnswerStore
});
```

```ts
interface EligibilityDeps {
  assessor: Assessor;           // assess(payload), stateBenefitTypes()
  resultHandler: ResultHandler; // handle({ checkId, answers, result })
  answerStore?: AnswerStore;    // load(checkId), save(checkId, partial), clear(checkId)
}
```

### Boundary rules

- Code in `src/eligibility/` may only import:
  - `@ministryofjustice/hmpps-forge/*`
  - Node built-ins
  - its own files

  That rules out `#src/*`, `#utils/*`, `#types/*`, and any `../` path that leaves the folder. An ESLint `no-restricted-imports` override scoped to the folder enforces this.
- Host code may only import from `src/eligibility/index.ts`. A second lint rule enforces this.
- Auth, check identity, storage, the CFE client, layout and completion are all injected through `createEligibilityPackage` options or `EligibilityDeps`.
- All user-facing text uses keys from `src/eligibility/locales/en.json`. These are ported from CCQ's `config/locales/en.yml`, including the variants by level of help and for a partner.

### How Forge is used

- The journey runs `Eligibility.LoadAnswers` on access. It loads **every** saved answer into Forge with `setAnswer`, and publishes a navigation map (`Data("nav")`, step code to next step) built from `stepsFor(answers)`. See spike S1 for why both are needed.
- Each step redirects with `redirect({ goto: Data("nav.<step code>") })`. Forge's reachability checking then follows the same map, so a step that `stepsFor(answers)` leaves out cannot be reached.
- A valid submit runs `Eligibility.SaveAnswers`, which saves the answers and rebuilds the map before the redirect is evaluated.
- The journey sets `reachability: { unreachableRedirect: "frontier" }`, so a user who opens a step too early is sent to the first unanswered relevant step.
- If `cannotUseService(answers)` is true after a submit, the user is redirected to the module's "cannot use this service" page.
- Check your answers submits to `Eligibility.RunAssessment`, which does the following in order:
  1. builds the payload from `relevantAnswers(answers)`
  2. calls `assessor.assess`
  3. calls `resultHandler.handle`
  4. clears the session answers
  5. redirects to `onComplete`
- Add-another lists (for example bank accounts) render one field per row with `Iterator.Map` over a row list that `LoadAnswers` publishes. Fields use flat codes such as `bank_accounts_0_amount`, and `SaveAnswers` stores the rows as a list. See spike S2.

### Session storage

- Answers are stored under `req.session.eligibility[checkId]`, so checks in different tabs do not collide.
- If CFE fails, answers stay in the session and the user can retry.
- The change-answers loop keeps a pending copy at `req.session.eligibility[checkId].pending`, as CCQ does. It is committed only when the loop completes.

### cla-operator-app integration

- Mount the module as a child of `caseJourney` (`src/journeys/inbound-call/journey.ts`) at `/case/:caseId/eligibility/...`. It inherits `LoadCase` and receives SiLAS auth through `onAccess`.
- Other changes outside the module:
  - register the package in `src/journeys/index.ts`
  - add `src/eligibility/views` to `utils/nunjucksSetup.ts`
  - merge `src/eligibility/locales` in `i18nLoader.ts`
  - add `src/eligibility/**/*.spec.ts` to `.mocharc.json`
  - make sure the Docker image ships `src/eligibility/views` and `locales`, since templates are read from disk at runtime
- End-to-end tests stay in `tests/playwright/`, with CFE stubbed through MSW.

